; A function the type system happily accepts and the termination checker
; refuses to bless: the recursion never descends, so no structural argument
; exists. The kernel labels it `termination unproven` rather than rejecting
; it — and when its oath runs, the fuel bound converts the infinite loop into an
; INDETERMINATE outcome instead of a hang: the case could not be evaluated, so
; per SPEC §4.1 it is a `no-verdict` and the definition is `asserted`.
;
; This comment said FALSIFIED until §4.1, and that was the conflation §4.1
; removed — the evaluator running out of fuel is a fact about the evaluator, not
; a counterexample to the property. `spin` remains an honest exhibit; what
; changed is which honest thing it exhibits.

(defn spin [] [(x Int)] Int
  (spin x)
  (prop claims-zero [(x Int)]
    (== (spin x) 0)))
